# AC3 — deterministic tie-break (NEARS-2989)

Dev DB has only 1 tipped order (dm_tips=40.00, count=1) -- no real tie exists to observe live, and
constructing one would mean writing rows into the shared dev `orders` table, which the QA hard
rule "read-only on the database" disallows (writes only via versioned seeder/migration after
approval -- out of scope here). Per the QA Test Scope's own fallback ("...or trust the engineer's
unit test test_tied_counts_return_the_lower_amount; read it and confirm it actually asserts what
it claims"), reviewed that test directly:

Admin/tests/Feature/Api/V1/Nears2989MostTipsControllerTest.php:48-59
- Seeds dm_tips=5.25 x2, dm_tips=3.75 x2 (genuine tie on max count=2), plus dm_tips=8.10 x1
  (strictly lower count, cannot win).
- Asserts response is EXACTLY {"most_tips_amount": 3.75} -- the LOWER of the two tied amounts.
- Also asserts the value is PHP float-typed (assertIsFloat), so this isn't a vacuous int-coerced
  check.
- Fractional (non-whole) amounts used deliberately so the assertion can't pass by accident.

Ran isolated: `vendor/bin/phpunit --filter Nears2989MostTipsControllerTest` -> 4/4 PASS (see
automated backstop). Query logic (ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC, dm_tips ASC LIMIT 1) is inherently
deterministic SQL, not order-dependent PHP array iteration -- the test's assertion is a faithful,
non-vacuous pin of the AC3 tie-break requirement.

Verdict: PASS by code+test review, live construction of a real tie in the shared dev DB
deliberately not attempted (DB read-only rule).
